CAT = "panceProf"; SEG = "PRO"
Q = [
("Before an elective procedure, which must be discussed for valid informed consent?",
 "Reasonable alternatives", ["The clinician's fee", "The hospital's ranking", "Other patients' outcomes"],
 "Informed consent covers the nature of the procedure, its risks and benefits, reasonable alternatives (including no treatment), and the chance to ask questions. It is given by a patient with decision-making capacity, freely."),
("A 52-year-old with decision-making capacity refuses a blood transfusion for religious reasons after a full discussion of the risks. What should the PA do?",
 "Respect her refusal", ["Give blood once she is sedated", "Ask her family to consent", "Seek a court order at once"],
 "A capacitated adult may refuse any treatment, even life-saving, which reflects respect for autonomy. The discussion, her understanding and alternatives offered are documented."),
("Under HIPAA, which disclosure of a patient's records needs no written authorization?",
 "To the treating cardiologist", ["To the patient's employer", "To a drug marketing firm", "To a reporter who calls"],
 "HIPAA allows sharing protected health information without authorization for treatment, payment and health care operations, so sending records to a treating specialist is permitted. Employers and marketers need authorization."),
("A PA sees a 3-year-old with patterned bruises in different stages of healing and a story that does not fit. What must the PA do?",
 "File a report with CPS", ["Wait for a second visit", "Ask the parent to explain", "Refer to a dermatologist"],
 "Every US state requires clinicians to report reasonable suspicion of child abuse; proof is not needed, and reporting in good faith is protected. The child's safety is addressed at the same time."),
("A test with very high sensitivity comes back negative. What does this result do?",
 "Helps rule out the disease", ["Helps rule in the disease", "Confirms a false positive", "Proves the disease is absent"],
 "High sensitivity means few false negatives, so a negative result helps rule out disease (SnNout). A highly specific test with a positive result helps rule it in (SpPin)."),
("In a study, 72 people with a disease test positive and 18 with the disease test negative. What is the test's sensitivity?",
 "80%", ["72%", "90%", "20%"],
 "Sensitivity is true positives over all with disease: 72 / (72 + 18) = 72 / 90 = 80%. 20% is the false negative rate."),
("A drug lowers the 5-year risk of stroke from 9% to 4%. What is the number needed to treat to prevent one stroke?",
 "20", ["5", "11", "25"],
 "The absolute risk reduction is 9% - 4% = 5%, and NNT = 1 / 0.05 = 20. 5 is the risk reduction itself, and 11 and 25 come from using one group's risk alone instead of the difference."),
("A PA realizes a patient received a tenfold overdose of a drug that caused no lasting harm. What is the right first step after making the patient safe?",
 "Tell the patient promptly", ["Wait for harm to appear", "Tell only the risk manager", "Change the record first"],
 "Medical errors are disclosed to the patient honestly and promptly, with an apology and the plan to prevent a repeat, and reported through the facility's safety system. Altering records is never acceptable."),
("A patient who speaks only Vietnamese needs a discussion about a new cancer diagnosis. Who should interpret?",
 "A trained medical interpreter", ["The patient's 12-year-old son", "A bilingual hospital visitor", "A translation app alone"],
 "Qualified medical interpreters improve accuracy, privacy and understanding; family members, and children in particular, are avoided for clinical discussions. Federal law requires language access in many settings."),
('Researchers follow 4,000 smokers and nonsmokers for 15 years and compare how many develop bladder cancer. What type of study is this?',
 'Prospective cohort study', ['Case-control study', 'Randomized controlled trial', 'Cross-sectional survey'],
 'A cohort study groups people by exposure and follows them forward to compare outcome rates, giving a relative risk. A case-control study starts with outcomes and looks back at exposures.'),
("Honoring an informed patient's choice to stop dialysis reflects which ethical principle?",
 "Autonomy", ["Beneficence", "Justice", "Nonmaleficence"],
 "Autonomy is respect for a capable patient's own decisions. Beneficence is acting for the patient's good, nonmaleficence is avoiding harm, and justice is fair distribution of care."),
("A patient without decision-making capacity has a durable power of attorney for health care. Who makes treatment decisions?",
 "The named health care agent", ["The oldest adult child", "The attending clinician", "The hospital ethics committee"],
 "A health care agent named in a durable power of attorney decides for the patient, guided by the patient's known wishes and advance directive. Default surrogate lists apply only when no agent was named."),
]
